  "summary": "New comparisons evaluate causal discovery on kinetic and CRISPR data while inference tools for intractable-likelihood and sparse-data models are consolidated.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is evaluation of inference methods for dynamical and regulatory networks under realistic data limits. One preprint benchmarks temporal causal discovery (tMIIC, PCMCI+/LPCMCI, SVAR-FCI) on simulated biochemical kinetic models with latent confounders and unobserved variables. A published study compares interventional causal structure learning algorithms on synthetic and CRISPR perturbation data for gene regulatory network inference. The other two items are looser fits: a tutorial on neural simulation-based inference for cognitive models with intractable likelihoods, and hierarchical dynamical surrogate models learned from sparse trust-game sequences that outperform RL and Bayesian baselines. What is new is the emphasis on partial observability, interventional data and head-to-head comparison against baselines rather than on new algorithms alone.",
